From b60bf148060973c927d2982dca0c2ce368209f5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 16:16:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(header): BLINK-01 - the header hid itself on every page,
 with nobody touching it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

REPRODUCED. Chromium, the shipped hook bundled with React against this
stylesheet, a page opened part-way down, and nothing touched for 1.4 seconds:

  BEFORE  HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN HIDDEN …
  AFTER   shown  shown  shown  shown  shown  shown  shown  shown  shown  …

The visitor sees the header paint, correct and present, and then slide away on
its own 350ms later. The smallest scroll up brings it back. That is the
blinking, and it happens on every page opened at a restored position — a
reload, the Back button, a link to a #section, or an App Router client
navigation. On a site people navigate by clicking, that is every page, every
time, on phones and on desktops, which is exactly what the calls describe.

THE CAUSE

The hook started at `lastY = 0` and compared the first sample against it:

    const [lastY, setLastY] = useState(0);
    …
    if (Math.abs(current - lastY) > 40) {
      setDirection(current > lastY && current > 220 ? 'down' : 'up');

A page restored at y=1605 delivers 1605 as its first reading. Measured against
zero that is 1,605 pixels of downward scrolling that never happened, so the bar
hid. Nobody had scrolled at all.

WHAT I CHECKED AND RULED OUT, BECAUSE A PLAUSIBLE FIX IS NOT A FIX

  · Ordinary scrolling does NOT blink. The shipped hook was driven over
    recorded gestures — steady scrolling, trackpad noise, momentum settle,
    deliberate reversals — and the header changed state 6 times across 1,520px
    of travel, 247px apart at the closest. Correct behaviour.
  · `backdrop-filter: saturate(200%) blur(20px)` on a 15%-transparent bar was
    the strongest competing theory and it is WRONG. With realistic page content
    behind it, 0.00% of the header's pixels changed per frame. An earlier 17%
    reading came from a striped test page in my own harness, not from the site.

Both of those were measured before this was written, and both would have
produced a confident, wrong patch.

THREE MORE DEFECTS IN THE SAME HOOK, FIXED BECAUSE THEY ARE ALSO WRONG

  1. The effect DEPENDED on the state it wrote: `useEffect(…, [lastY])`. The
     scroll listener was torn down and re-added every 40px, and the `ticking`
     flag that throttles the rAF is a local inside that effect — so every
     re-subscription minted a fresh `ticking = false` and defeated its own
     throttle. Several callbacks could be in flight, each closed over a
     different `lastY`, each writing an anchor computed from a stale one.
  2. `window.scrollY` was read unclamped. iOS reports NEGATIVE values while
     rubber-banding at the top, and the sign of `current > lastY` flips during
     the bounce.
  3. `scrolled` toggled on one threshold (`> 16`), so a reader resting there
     strobes the background, the border and the box-shadow — three transitions
     — over a backdrop-filter that repaints each time.

WHAT REPLACES IT

lib/scroll-state.ts: the decision as a PURE FUNCTION, with an anchor that
tracks the furthest point reached in the current direction and flips only on a
genuine 64px reversal. Nothing in the hot path is React state — the anchor is a
ref, the effect subscribes exactly once, and state is written only when the bar
would actually look different, so scrolling no longer re-renders the header
sixty times a second.

The first sample now only ever ESTABLISHES the anchor. It can never hide the
bar. My own first draft of this fix got that wrong in precisely the same way as
the shipped code, which is why it is a named case in the file and an asserted
one in the test.

SO THAT IT NEVER HAPPENS AGAIN

scroll-state.test.ts, 30 assertions, all run and passing. Because the decision
is pure, the gestures replay without a browser: opening at six different depths
must never hide the bar; trackpad wobble over 200 samples must produce zero
movement; momentum settle, noisy scrolling and the full iOS rubber-band
sequence including negative positions must produce zero movement; a deliberate
reversal must still work; the dead band must not toggle for a reader sitting
inside it.

One of those assertions failed the first time it was run — the rubber-band case
counted the legitimate null→'up' transition on the first sample — and it is
fixed with the reason written down rather than the expectation quietly relaxed.

VERIFIED

  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css fails only in this sandbox for want of
  node_modules and passed in the Codespace on the last four runs.
  All 30 scroll-state assertions executed here and passing.
  Header.tsx, scroll-state.ts and scroll-state.test.ts all parse clean.
  Before/after reproduced in headless Chromium, sampled 14 times over 1.4s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
